en.math:
\begin{aligned}
\Delta t^2-\Delta x^2 &>0 &&\text{timelike},\\
\Delta t^2-\Delta x^2 &=0 &&\text{lightlike},\\
\Delta t^2-\Delta x^2 &<0 &&\text{spacelike}.\end{aligned}

p.src:
Why does Eq.  equal a clock reading? For a clock at rest, $\Delta x=0$, so $\Delta\tau=\Delta t$. For a uniformly moving clock, choose the primed frame in which that clock is at rest. There $\Delta x'=0$, and invariance gives $\Delta\tau
=\sqrt{\Delta t'^2-\Delta x'^2}
=\Delta t'.$ Moving clocks therefore tick off proper time.

